$f(x)=|(2 x-1)(x+2)|+\frac{\sin 2 x}{2} 0 \leq x<\frac{1}{2} \quad f(x)=(1-2 x)(x+2)+\frac{\sin 2 x}{2} f^{\prime}(x)=-4 x-3+\cos 2 x<0$



For $x \geq \frac{1}{2}: \quad f^{\prime}(x)=4 x+3+\cos 2 x>0$



So, minima occurs at $x=\frac{1}{2}  \begin{aligned}
\left.f(x)\right|$_{\min } &=$\left|2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)$^{2}+\frac{3}{2}-2\right|+$\sin \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \cdot \cos \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)$ \\\\
&=\frac{1}{2} \sin 1
\end{aligned} 



So, maxima is possible at $x=0$ or $x=1$



Now checking for $x=0$ and $x=1$, we can see it attains its maximum value at $x=1  \begin{aligned}
\left.f(x)\right|$_{\max }=&|2+3-2|+\frac{\sin 2}{2} \\\\
&=3+\frac{1}{2} \sin 2
\end{aligned} 



